  <!-- 7 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · In code</p>
      <h2>DFS in Java</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>DFS is short when written with recursion. This program builds the main example and runs DFS from A.</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code)} /></pre>
      <TryIt cls="DFS" />
      <p>Most lines match the BFS program (see its <a href={base + '/itc120/bfs/#java'}>read-aloud table</a>). Three lines to read aloud:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl ra">
        <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>static boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];</code></td><td>"make n true/false slots named visited, all <code>false</code> at first." It is <code>static</code> (outside the method) so every recursive call of <code>dfs</code> shares the same array.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>adj[u][v] == 1 &amp;&amp; !visited[v]</code></td><td>"v is a neighbor of u <b>and</b> v is <b>not</b> visited yet."</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>dfs(v);</code></td><td>"go deeper: run dfs on v all the way, then come back and continue this loop."</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Match the code to the procedure</p>
        <p>Calling <code>dfs(v)</code> is the <b>push</b> (go deeper). When the <code>for</code> loop ends, the method returns: that is the <b>pop</b> (backtrack). The <code>visited</code> array stops us from going around a cycle forever. Compare with BFS: there is no queue here, because Java's call stack is the stack.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow" id="task">
        <p class="note-label">Your coding task: count what DFS can reach</p>
        <p>In <Peek label="Part 4" title="From Part 4 · What can be reached?" html={reachPeek} />, DFS from A reached 5 vertices, but DFS from F reached only 3. Now write a DFS that counts them.</p>
        <p>Finish <code>countReach</code> in about 8 lines where it says YOUR CODE. It is the <code>dfs</code> method from this part with a count added. Each call counts its own vertex (1), plus everything its deeper calls reach. Like <a href={base + '/itc120/recursion/#task'}>height in the Recursion lesson</a>, a recursive call gives back a number, and you add it. The graph is the one-way graph, built with <code>addArrow</code> (one direction only). <code>main</code> clears <code>visited</code> before each new test. Run it: each printed line must match its expected comment.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt />
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show one possible answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskAns)} /></pre>
          <p>Output, one per line: <code>5</code>, <code>3</code>, <code>1</code>, <code>5</code>.</p>
          <p>Here is the first test, from A. The deepest calls finish first:</p>
          <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
            <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Why</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(C)</code></td><td>C's only neighbor, A, is already visited</td><td>1</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(B)</code></td><td>B itself + C</td><td>1 + 1 = 2</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(E)</code></td><td>no arrows go out of E</td><td>1</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(D)</code></td><td>D itself + E</td><td>1 + 1 = 2</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(A)</code></td><td>A itself + B's 2 + D's 2</td><td>1 + 2 + 2 = 5</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>The last test starts at B and also reaches 5: B → C → A, then A → D → E. The arrow C → A leads back to A. Only F is missed, because no arrow points into F.</p>
          <p>Here are two common mistakes:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Writing only <code>countReach(v);</code>. The call still runs, but the number it gives back is thrown away. Every call returns 1, so every test prints <code>1</code>.</li>
            <li>Forgetting <code>visited[u] = true</code>. Then DFS goes A → B → C → A → B → … around the cycle forever. Each call waits for the next one, and Java's call stack fills up: the program stops with <code>StackOverflowError</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

